Project-Testing
Test Planning
The first step is to prepare a testing plan that defines what needs to be tested and how it will be tested.
Objectives:
•	Verify that all EduGenie features work correctly.
•	Ensure users receive relevant educational responses.
•	Check communication between EduGenie and the Gemini API.
•	Identify and fix errors before deployment.
•	Verify that invalid or incomplete user inputs are handled properly.
Testing areas:
•	User interface
•	User registration/login, if implemented
•	Question/input processing
•	Gemini API integration
•	AI-generated answers
•	Quiz generation
•	Summarization and explanation features
•	Error handling
•	Security
•	Performance
•	Database/storage, if used
2. Unit Testing
Unit testing checks individual modules independently.
For EduGenie, individual functions such as the following can be tested:
Module	Example Test
Login	Verify valid and invalid credentials
User Input	Check whether empty questions are rejected
Gemini API	Verify that requests are sent correctly
Response Processing	Check whether Gemini responses are displayed correctly
Quiz Generator	Verify that questions and options are generated
Summarizer	Check whether submitted text produces a summary
UI Components	Verify buttons, text boxes, menus, etc.
Example:
Test Case: Empty question submission
Input: User clicks "Ask" without entering a question
Expected Result: System displays a message asking the user to enter a question instead of sending an unnecessary API request.
3. Integration Testing
Integration testing verifies that different EduGenie components work together correctly.
For example:
User → EduGenie Interface → Backend → Gemini API → Response Processing → User Interface
The following integrations should be tested:
•	Frontend with backend
•	Backend with Gemini API
•	Authentication with user data, if applicable
•	Database with application, if applicable
•	AI response with the user-interface display
Example:
A student enters "Explain photosynthesis in simple words." The system should send the request to Gemini, receive the response, process it correctly, and display the explanation without formatting or connection errors.
4. Gemini API Testing
Because EduGenie depends on Google Gemini, API-related testing is an important part of the project.
The following cases should be tested:
•	Valid API request
•	Invalid API key
•	Missing API key
•	Network interruption
•	API timeout
•	Empty Gemini response
•	Excessively long user input
•	API rate-limit/error response
•	Unexpected API response format
Expected behavior:
When the Gemini service is unavailable, EduGenie should show a meaningful error message rather than crashing.
5. Functional Testing
Functional testing verifies whether EduGenie performs the functions specified in the requirements.
Typical test scenarios include:
1.	User opens EduGenie.
2.	User enters a learning question.
3.	User submits the question.
4.	EduGenie sends the request to Gemini.
5.	Gemini generates a response.
6.	EduGenie displays the response.
7.	User can continue asking questions.
Other functionality can include:
•	Generate explanations
•	Generate summaries
•	Generate quizzes
•	Provide examples
•	Answer follow-up questions
•	Handle different subjects
•	Clear/reset conversations
•	Save learning history, if implemented
6. AI Response Testing
AI-specific testing is required because a technically correct application can still produce an unsuitable educational response.
Responses should be checked for:
•	Relevance: Does the answer address the user's question?
•	Correctness: Is the educational information factually appropriate?
•	Clarity: Is it understandable to the target learners?
•	Completeness: Does it adequately answer the question?
•	Consistency: Do similar questions produce reasonably consistent answers?
•	Instruction following: Does the assistant follow the requested format?
•	Hallucination: Does it invent facts, references, or explanations?
For example, if the user asks:
"Explain Newton's First Law for a school student."
The response should provide an understandable explanation rather than an unrelated or unnecessarily advanced answer.
7. Usability Testing
Usability testing evaluates how easily students and other intended users can operate EduGenie.
Test users can be asked to perform tasks such as:
•	Ask a question.
•	Request a simpler explanation.
•	Generate a quiz.
•	Read an AI response.
•	Start another question.
•	Recover from an error.
Feedback can be collected regarding:
•	Ease of navigation
•	Readability
•	Interface design
•	Response speed
•	Ease of entering questions
•	Understanding of AI responses
•	Overall user experience
8. Performance Testing
Performance testing determines how EduGenie behaves under different workloads.
Important measurements include:
•	Page/application loading time
•	Time required to send an AI request
•	Time taken to display Gemini's response
•	API response handling
•	Application behavior with multiple users
•	Large input handling
The system should remain responsive and should handle temporary delays from the Gemini API gracefully.
9. Security Testing
Security testing ensures that user information and API credentials are protected.
Tests should verify:
•	API keys are not exposed in frontend code.
•	Unauthorized users cannot access protected features.
•	User inputs are handled safely.
•	Sensitive information is not unnecessarily stored.
•	Authentication works correctly, if implemented.
•	Error messages do not reveal confidential configuration details.
10. Negative Testing
Negative testing checks how the system behaves when users provide invalid or unexpected inputs.
Examples:
Test Input	Expected Behavior
Empty question	Display validation message
Very long question	Handle or restrict input appropriately
Special characters	Process safely
Unsupported request	Provide an appropriate response
No internet connection	Display connection error
Gemini API unavailable	Display service-unavailable message
Invalid authentication	Reject access
11. System Testing
System testing evaluates the complete EduGenie application as one integrated system.
A typical end-to-end test is:
Open application → Login → Enter question → Send request → Gemini processes request → Response received → Response displayed → User continues learning
The complete workflow should be tested on the intended devices, browsers, and operating environments.
12. User Acceptance Testing (UAT)
User Acceptance Testing is performed using representative users, such as students or teachers.
Users verify whether the application satisfies the project's requirements.
Sample UAT activities:
•	Ask subject-related questions.
•	Request simplified explanations.
•	Generate practice questions.
•	Generate quizzes.
•	Test follow-up questions.
•	Check whether responses are easy to understand.
•	Report usability problems.
The feedback is documented and necessary corrections are made before final deployment.
13. Regression Testing
After fixing bugs, previously tested features should be tested again.
For example, if changes are made to the Gemini API integration, testers should verify that:
•	Question submission still works.
•	Responses are still displayed.
•	Quiz generation still works.
•	Error handling still works.
•	Existing UI functionality has not been affected.
This prevents new changes from breaking existing functionality.
14. Test Documentation
All testing activities should be documented using test cases.
A typical test-case format is:
Test Case ID	Module	Test Scenario	Expected Result	Actual Result	Status
TC-01	Login	Enter valid credentials	User logged in	As expected	Pass
TC-02	Question	Enter valid question	Gemini response displayed	As expected	Pass
TC-03	Input	Submit empty question	Validation message displayed	As expected	Pass
TC-04	API	Gemini API unavailable	Error message displayed	As expected	Pass
TC-05	Quiz	Request quiz	Quiz generated	As expected	Pass
TC-06	UI	Open application on mobile	Interface displayed properly	As expected	Pass
15. Bug Identification and Fixing
Any failures found during testing should be recorded in a bug report.
A bug report can contain:
•	Bug ID
•	Module
•	Description
•	Steps to reproduce
•	Expected result
•	Actual result
•	Severity
•	Status
•	Resolution
Example:
Bug ID: BUG-01
Module: Gemini API
Problem: Response is not displayed when the API request times out.
Expected: A user-friendly timeout message should be displayed.
Status: Fixed and retested.
16. Final Testing and Approval
After all identified defects are fixed, final testing is performed.
The project can be considered ready for deployment when:
•	Major functional requirements have passed.
•	Critical bugs have been resolved.
•	Gemini API integration works correctly.
•	AI responses meet the defined quality requirements.
•	Security checks are completed.
•	Usability testing is satisfactory.
•	Regression testing is completed.
•	User acceptance testing is completed.
Testing Phase Summary
The testing phase of EduGenie: Google Gemini Powered Learning Assistant ensures that the system is functionally correct, reliable, secure, user-friendly, and capable of providing appropriate AI-powered educational assistance. Testing is performed at multiple levels, including unit testing, integration testing, functional testing, AI response testing, usability testing, performance testing, security testing, system testing, UAT, and regression testing. The results are documented through test cases and bug reports, and the system is retested after corrections before final deployment.

